<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charondas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charondas was a lawgiver of the Greek city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, probably in the 6th century BCE. His laws, known for their detailed provisions on family, property and contracts, were adopted by other Chalcidian colonies in Sicily and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including Rhegion. Ancient writers told the story that he killed himself after breaking his own law by entering the assembly armed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charondas.jpg" alt="Charondas" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eighteenth century French print illustrator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_the_death_of_Charondas_from_1787.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
